    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter May 5th with a primary objective of capitalizing on speed and favorable matchups, headlined by our strongest plays: **Trea Turner** (75.88) and **Kyle Schwarber** (72.47). Both benefit immensely from facing Severino, whose 4.59 xERA provides the most exploitable pitching window on the card today. **Trea Turner** is the anchor of our lineup, as his leadoff status paired with high-volume stolen base potential against a vulnerable arm makes him an elite play. Similarly, **Kyle Schwarber** is well-positioned to drive in runs at the top of the order, with our analytical model rewarding his strong xwOBA of 0.396. **Chase Meidroth** (66.48) also emerges as a high-floor selection; batting leadoff against Aldegheri\u2019s 5.50 xERA creates a perfect storm for on-base production and stolen base opportunities.\n\nNavigating the remainder of the roster, **Dillon Dingler** (63.75) benefits from a favorable platoon advantage against Mor\u00e1n, helping push his score into the \"Strong\" category. While **Yordan Alvarez** (58.67) faces an elite matchup against Ohtani, his raw talent and batting order placement ensure he remains in the starting lineup despite the clear SP risk. **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (56.20) was the closest player to being benched; while his sprint speed and the slow delivery of Abbott, A make him a high-upside play for steals, the negative impact of his #9 spot in the order and a challenging platoon dynamic kept him on the fringe of our \"Strong\" tier. \n\nRegarding roster depth, we continue to monitor the status of **Christian Yelich**, who remains sidelined on the IL, alongside **Hyeseong Kim** and **Colt Keith**, both of whom are currently out of their respective starting lineups. **Zac Veen** remains in the minors with the Albuquerque Isotopes, leaving him unavailable for selection regardless of his potential upside. We are keeping a close watch on the rotation, and should opportunities arise for **Lars Nootbaar**, *